From e9dbf12cd6670050d16d2e5df80f54ad46447370 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Junod <donujp@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 14:13:56 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Autogain: make the libebur128 state safe to use from several
 threads

The libebur128 state of the autogain plugin is created on the plugin worker
thread, used by the realtime thread in process(), and changed from the user
interface thread when the maximum history is edited. Only the last two held
the plugin mutex. The worker destroyed and replaced the state, and read
ebur128_ready and wrote old_rate, without it, so changing the maximum
history while the state was being rebuilt could use a state that was being
freed. Two rebuilds queued by two quick sample rate changes could also end
with the first one publishing its state and the second one returning early
on ebur128_ready, leaving a state for the old rate in use.

Move the state into autogain::AnalyserState, which is the only way to get at
it. The mutex of that class covers the creation, the history length and the
use of the state, and process() holds a lease on it for the whole call. A
rebuild is requested with a generation number and is published only if it is
still the latest one, so a state built for an old rate, or before the history
was reset, can no longer replace a newer one. The state is allocated by the
worker outside of the lock, and old states are freed outside of it.

Also:

- check the return values of ebur128_init(), ebur128_set_channel(),
  ebur128_set_max_history() and ebur128_add_frames_float(). A failed
  initialization used to be dereferenced, and the plugin was marked ready
  even if the channel setup failed;
- make resetHistory() hold the plugin mutex, and reset prev_gain together
  with the output gain. Only the latter was reset, so the first block after
  a reset smoothed from the gain of the history that had just been discarded.

The new autogain_analyser_test covers the ownership rules with a fake state
type, so it does not need libebur128.
